1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,880
Proposition thirty-seven creates a loan program for buyers of

2
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:08,140
qualifying new homes.

3
00:00:08,660 --> 00:00:11,480
The problem it targets is the upfront cash needed

4
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:13,920
to buy, not the entire cost of housing.

5
00:00:14,540 --> 00:00:17,080
Assistance would be another loan, not free money.

6
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:20,600
The state housing finance agency could issue up to

7
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:23,280
twenty-five billion dollars in revenue bonds.

8
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:26,680
Homebuyers’ payments would repay investors and administration.

9
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:30,620
The analyst finds no direct state or local cost

10
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:31,360
under this structure.

11
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:34,680
Imagine a family budgeting for a newly built home.

12
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:38,713
For an illustrative home costing six hundred thousand dollars,

13
00:00:38,713 --> 00:00:41,360
three percent down is eighteen thousand dollars.

14
00:00:42,140 --> 00:00:46,010
A loan covering seventeen percent would be one hundred

15
00:00:46,010 --> 00:00:47,020
two thousand.

16
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:51,420
The remaining eighty percent is four hundred eighty thousand.

17
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:54,120
That can reduce cash needed at closing, but the

18
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:57,620
family still owes the assistance loan as well as

19
00:00:57,620 --> 00:00:58,440
its main mortgage.

20
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,780
Affordability depends on both monthly payments, interest rates, taxes,

21
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:05,060
insurance, and other costs.

22
00:01:05,420 --> 00:01:07,940
The program is tied to qualifying new housing.

23
00:01:08,580 --> 00:01:13,540
Borrowers must meet one year of California residency, owner-occupancy,

24
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:17,900
and income rules, including an income ceiling tied to

25
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:19,300
twice the area’s median.

26
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:23,380
Homes must be qualifying first sales, with county-based price

27
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:26,570
limits roughly around one million to one and a

28
00:01:26,570 --> 00:01:28,460
half million dollars, adjusted annually.

29
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:30,260
How many loans could it fund?

30
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:33,320
Using the example loan amount, the bond could support

31
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:36,540
roughly two hundred and forty-five thousand loans.

32
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:40,240
That is arithmetic, not an official forecast; actual loan

33
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:42,460
sizes, issuance, and costs matter.

34
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:47,140
Developers can build eligible homes without using the optional

35
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:48,640
qualified-builder route.

36
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:53,460
That option pairs higher labor standards with different construction-defect

37
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:54,020
rules.

38
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:57,640
The measure does not directly guarantee a particular number

39
00:01:57,640 --> 00:01:59,140
of homes will be built.

40
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:03,020
A voter might support helping households with adequate income

41
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:05,080
but too little saved cash.

42
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:09,280
Restricting the assistance to new homes could encourage construction,

43
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:13,400
connecting stronger purchasing power to additional supply.

44
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:16,500
Whether developers respond enough is uncertain.

45
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:20,620
You might ask whether easier buying pushes prices up.

46
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:24,560
If more buyers can bid while construction stays constrained,

47
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:27,920
some benefit can be absorbed into higher prices.

48
00:02:28,580 --> 00:02:32,480
New-home eligibility helps link the program to supply, but

49
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:36,000
does not remove land, labor, or permitting constraints.

50
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:40,780
Easier financing alone is not a complete affordability solution.

51
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:44,800
A skeptical voter could also question the extra household

52
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:47,740
debt or the construction-defect tradeoff.

53
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:51,760
No direct government cost does not mean every borrower

54
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:54,500
benefits, or that every risk disappears.

55
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:58,380
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 61

56
00:02:58,380 --> 00:03:02,710
percent said yes, 35 percent no, and 3 percent

57
00:03:02,710 --> 00:03:02,960
were unsure.

58
00:03:03,720 --> 00:03:07,220
These are dated survey results, not votes already cast.

59
00:03:07,980 --> 00:03:12,380
Yes creates this loan program; no leaves existing assistance

60
00:03:12,380 --> 00:03:15,500
options in place without requiring the new program.

61
